      "chunkSize": 10,
      "rawToolArgs": "{\"comparisons\":[{\"requirementIndex\":0,\"lemmaName\":\"array_sum\",\"match\":true,\"discrepancy\":\"\",\"weakeningType\":\"none\",\"explanation\":\"The lemma faithfully captures the requirement. It ensures that the returned array c has the same length as the inputs and that each element c[i] equals a[i] + b[i], which is exactly the pair-wise sum operation requested.\"},{\"requirementIndex\":1,\"lemmaName\":\"below_zero__ctrl_forall_to_not_exists\",\"match\":true,\"discrepancy\":\"\",\"weakeningType\":\"none\",\"explanation\":\"The lemma correctly captures all aspects of the requirement. It ensures the presum array has the correct length and starts with 0, that consecutive elements follow the cumulative sum property, and that the result is true iff a negative balance exists and false iff all balances are non-negative.\"},{\"requirementIndex\":2,\"lemmaName\":\"binary_search__ctrl_contrapositive\",\"match\":false,\"discrepancy\":\"The requirement specifies 'array[index-1] < key <= array[index]', which means the returned index should satisfy: all elements before it are strictly less than the key, and the element at that index (if it exists) should be >= key. The lemma ensures all elements before n are < key and all elements from n onward are >= key, but it does not explicitly ensure that array[n] >= key when n < a.Length. The postcondition 'forall i :: n <= i < a.Length ==> a[i] >= key' is vacuously true when n == a.Length, which doesn't guarantee a[n] >= key when n is in bounds.\",\"weakeningType\":\"weakened-postcondition\",\"explanation\":\"The lemma's postcondition is weaker than the requirement. When n < a.Length, the requirement demands a[n] >= key, but the lemma only ensures this indirectly through the range quantifier. The third postcondition about contrapositive doesn't add the missing guarantee.\"},{\"requirementIndex\":3,\"lemmaName\":\"linear_search2__C6\",\"match\":true,\"discrepancy\":\"\",\"weakeningType\":\"none\",\"explanation\":\"The lemma correctly captures the requirement. It ensures that the returned index n is within bounds...[truncated]"
    }
